relicnos-c2811 is licensed per directory.  In short: the userspace tools and
documentation are MIT, and the kernel sources are GPL-2.0 because they have to
be.

  tools/, docs/, initramfs/, README.md      MIT          LICENSES/MIT.txt
  kernel/                                   GPL-2.0      LICENSES/GPL-2.0.txt
  firmware/                                 not ours     see firmware/README.md

Every source file carries an SPDX-License-Identifier saying which applies.


Why the kernel part is not permissive
-------------------------------------

kernel/cisco2811/ is a Linux board port and device driver.  It is compiled into
the kernel, includes kernel headers, and calls internal kernel APIs, which
makes it a derivative work of Linux; nm32a.c already declares
MODULE_LICENSE("GPL").  It cannot be relicensed under permissive terms by us,
and pretending otherwise would not help anyone downstream.

This is the ordinary arrangement for out-of-tree drivers, and it is not a
practical restriction for the intended use: build it, run it, modify it, ship
appliances with it.  If you distribute a modified kernel you must offer the
kernel source, which you would have to do for Linux itself regardless.

The parts most likely to be reused elsewhere -- the microcode extractor, the
console server, the register tools -- are all MIT and carry no such condition.


firmware/
---------

firmware/cd2481_ucode.bin and firmware/nm_as16_fw.bin are Cisco firmware,
extracted unmodified from an IOS image for interoperability: the CD2481 UARTs
have no protocol code in ROM and the hardware does not function without the
microcode.  They are not covered by either licence above and no claim is made
over them.  tools/extract_cd2481_ucode.py reproduces the microcode from your
own IOS image if you would rather not rely on the copy here.
